# Project Structure Guide

This project follows a modular structure, especially suited for NestJS applications. Use this guide when creating new modules to ensure consistency.

## Main Directories

- [`src/`](mdc:src/): Main source code directory.
  - [`app.module.ts`](mdc:src/app.module.ts): Root module of the application.
  - [`modules/`](mdc:src/modules/): Contains all feature modules. **New modules should be created here.**
    - Each module should have its own folder, e.g., `users/`, `auth/`, etc.
    - Inside each module folder:
      - `module-name.module.ts`: The module definition.
      - `module-name.controller.ts`: Controller(s) for the module.
      - `module-name.service.ts`: Service(s) for the module.
      - `module-name.entity.ts` (if applicable): Entity definitions.
      - `module-name.dto.ts` (if applicable): DTOs for data validation/transfer.
      - `module-name.response.ts` (if applicable): Response types for the module.
      - `module-name.constants.ts` (if applicable): Constants for the module.
      - `module-name.helpers.ts` (if applicable): Helper functions for the module.
      - `module-name.types.ts` (if applicable): Type definitions for the module.
      - `module-name.utils.ts` (if applicable): Utility functions for the module.
      - `module-name.validator.service.ts` (if applicable): Validator functions for the module.
  - [`config/`](mdc:src/config/): Application and environment configuration files.
  - [`constants/`](mdc:src/constants/): Application-wide constants, enums, etc.
  - [`decorators/`](mdc:src/decorators/): Custom decorators which are used in the project.
    - Define new decorators in this directory based on the project needs which will be used commonly across the project.
  - [`filters/`](mdc:src/filters/): Exception filters.
  - [`guards/`](mdc:src/guards/): Define Guards for handling the request/response.
  - [`interceptors/`](mdc:src/interceptors/): Interceptors for request/response handling.
  - [`services/`](mdc:src/services/): Shared services (e.g., logger, database, stripe, notification, etc.).
    - Define new services in this directory based on the project needs which will be used commonly across the project.
    - If a service is used in a single module, it should be placed in the module directory.
  - [`types/`](mdc:src/types/): Type definitions common across the project.
  - [`utils/`](mdc:src/utils/): Utility functions common across the project.
  - [`worker/`](mdc:src/worker/): It's separate application for running the workers. And it will be mainly used for running the side tasks which will help to reduce the load on the main application.
    - Mainly cron jobs will be defined here.

## Module Creation Guidelines

- Place new modules in [`src/modules/`](mdc:src/modules/).
- Follow the naming convention: `module-name/` for the folder, and `module-name.module.ts`, etc., for files.
- Register new modules in [`app.module.ts`](mdc:src/app.module.ts) if they should be part of the main app.
- (if applicable): Import newly created entity inside the `DatabaseEntityModule` in `src/modules/database/database-entity.module.ts` file.
- Controller file should call service functions and can not have any business logic.
- Define new DTOs based on the entity and service needs and give proper validation rules for the data.

## Naming Conventions

### Directories

- Use kebab-case (e.g., `module-name`, `module-name-2`)

### Files

- Use kebab-case for directory names
- Use PascalCase with type suffix for files:
  - `*.controller.ts`
  - `*.service.ts`
  - `*.entity.ts`
  - `*.module.ts`
  - `*.constants.ts`
  - `*.response.ts`
  - `*.validate.service.ts`
  - `*.dto.ts`

### Classes/Interfaces

- Use PascalCase (e.g., `EmployeePayService`, `CreateEmployeePayDto`)

### Variables/Methods

- Use camelCase (e.g., `variableNameId`, `variableName`)

### Constants

- Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for constant objects
- Group related constants in objects (e.g., `SUCCESS_MESSAGES`)

## Entity Implementation

### Base Entity Extension

```typescript
@Entity("ModuleName")
export class ModuleNameEntity extends BaseEntity {
  // Entity-specific columns

  @BeforeInsert()
  async generateUuid() {
    this.id = await generateKSUID("prefix");
  }
}
```

### Best Practices

- Extend `BaseEntity` for audit fields (createdAt, updatedAt, deletedAt, createdBy, updatedBy)
- Use `@BeforeInsert()` with `generateKSUID()` for ID generation
- Use descriptive column types (`varchar`, `decimal`, `boolean`, `timestamp`)
- Include proper relationships with `@ManyToOne`, `@OneToOne`, `@JoinColumn`
- Use nullable properties with `nullable: true` where appropriate
- Add comments for business-critical fields using `// !!!!`
- `prefix` is a small string which is used to generate the ID and should not exceed the 3 characters. It should be unique for all the entities. (eg: Entity name is `User` then `prefix` should be `usr`)

## DTO Implementation

### Structure

```typescript
export class CreateModuleNameDto {
  @ApiProperty({ description: "Field description", example: "example value" })
  @IsString()
  @IsNotEmpty()
  fieldName: string;

  @ApiPropertyOptional({ description: "Optional field", nullable: true })
  @IsString()
  @IsOptional()
  optionalField?: string;

  @ApiProperty({ type: [NestedDto] })
  @IsArray()
  @ArrayNotEmpty()
  @ValidateNested({ each: true })
  @Type(() => NestedDto)
  @IsOptional()
  nestedArray?: NestedDto[];
}

export class UpdateModuleNameDto extends PartialType(CreateModuleNameDto) {}
```

### Validation Rules

- Use class-validator decorators (`@IsString()`, `@IsNumber()`, `@IsNotEmpty()`, `@IsOptional()` etc.)
- Include Swagger decorators (`@ApiProperty()`, `@ApiPropertyOptional()`)
- Use `@Type()` for nested objects and arrays
- Use `@ValidateNested()` for complex nested validation
- Use enums with `@IsEnum()` for controlled values
- If needed create multiple DTO files for the same entity based on the needs by creating dtos folder inside the module directory.

## Database Operations

### Transaction Management

- Use `DatabaseService.createQueryRunner()` for transactions
- Always include `queryRunner.commitTransaction()` and `queryRunner.rollbackTransaction()`
- Use `queryRunner.release()` in finally block
- Pass `{ data: { userId } }` for audit tracking

### Query Patterns

- Use QueryBuilder for complex queries
- Include proper select fields from constants
- Use parameterized queries to prevent SQL injection
- Implement soft delete where appropriate

